  "review": false,
  "text": "**English**\n\n- The **Thirukkural**, by Thiruvalluvar, is one of the *Pathinen Kilkanakku* (18 minor works). It has 1,330 couplets in 133 chapters of 10 couplets each, arranged in three books: **Aram** (virtue, ch. 1–38), **Porul** (wealth/polity, ch. 39–108) and **Kamam/Inbam** (love, ch. 109–133).\n- **Ozhukkamudaimai** is **chapter 14**, in the *Illaraviyal* (domestic virtue, ch. 5–24) section of Aram. It covers couplets **131–140**.\n- **Core idea:** good conduct brings distinction, so guard it more than life (131). Conduct brings greatness and its lapse brings disgrace (137). Good conduct is the seed of goodness and bad conduct brings sorrow (138). Those who have not learnt to live in harmony with the world are ignorant, however learned (140).\n- **Secular character:** the chapter speaks only of human conduct, not of any religion. This links to GS Unit VI (Group II/IIA): \"Thirukkural – significance as a secular literature, relevance to everyday life, universal values\".\n- **Exam use:** questions can ask you to complete a couplet, name the chapter or its number, match a meaning, split a compound word (*pirithu ezhuthuthal*), or give an antonym."
